// @error an interpolation hole is `string` or i32 | i64 | boolean | f64 — this one is P
// @error-at 19
// A HOLE OUTSIDE THE DOMAIN GETS A HOLE-SHAPED REFUSAL, AT THE HOLE — the same
// sentence in either quoted form, since they are the same construct.
//
// The desugared render call is a call the author never wrote, over a parameter
// list they cannot see, so the ordinary `argument 1: expected …, got P` would
// name nothing they can act on. The message names the domain instead, and
// anchors at the hole's own span.
//
// THE DOMAIN IN THE SENTENCE IS READ OFF THE RENDERER, not spelled in the
// compiler — which is why this line says `i32 | i64 | boolean | f64` and said
// `i32 | i64 | boolean` a week ago, with no compiler edit in between (serde
// Stage 0's `renderF64` widened it; `hole-f64-rides-the-renderer.vl` pins that
// half). A struct is outside it at any width: a record has no rendering at all
// and will not get one from a number renderer. `show<T>` — serde Stage 2 — is
// where a general one belongs, and the domain will widen there the same way.
type P = { x: i32 }
const p: P = { x: 1 }
print(`v=\{p}`)
